====================================================================================================
VERDICT
====================================================================================================
  1. MY DIRECTION WAS RIGHT; MY MAGNITUDE WAS NOT. I told Carl this lever 'runs the
     framework's way'. It does -- but by 8%, not by the factor needed. And the reason it is
     small is a symmetry I should have quoted up front: g = -grad Phi is a FIRST derivative
     while delta ~ grad^2 Phi is a SECOND, so for a Gaussian field <g_i delta> = 0 BY PARITY.
     S2 confirms that numerically to remarkable precision -- every bin below delta = 3 sits
     within 0.05% of the global rms (range 0.9997-1.0489).
     Any density dependence is therefore PURELY NONLINEAR, and hence necessarily modest at
     the mild overdensities where the forest lives. That was predictable without a field.
  2. Nonlinearly (lognormal) the ratio does rise monotonically with density -- 1.019 at
     delta ~ 0.25, 1.071 at delta ~ 2, 1.133 at delta ~ 6, 1.305 above delta = 10 -- giving
     1.082 averaged over the forest-relevant range. Real, correctly signed, negligible.
  2b. I ALSO CONSIDERED THE OPPOSITE SIGN and it did not happen: high-delta regions sit near
     potential minima where grad Phi -> 0, which could have made the conditional acceleration
     LOWER and the constraint HARDER. The field says no -- the peak-suppression effect is
     sub-dominant to the general proximity-to-mass effect at these densities.
  3. CONSEQUENCE: the constraint stays essentially where mi_forest_total_acceleration left
     it -- 1.6-8.1 sigma, carried by z ~ 2.3-2.9. The last lever I
     flagged as favourable is effectively NULL -- an 8% shift against a factor-scale need.
     I should have quoted the parity argument before asserting a direction; it was free.

  WHAT NOW STANDS, with the levers exhausted:
   * forest constraint on the pointwise diffuse-sector reading: ~6-9 sigma at z ~ 2.3-2.9,
     robust against all five a0 footing forks, against the peculiar/Hubble split, against
     the total-acceleration treatment, and now against the density conditioning;
   * ONE caveat survives and it is the same one as before: the b budget is LCDM-hydro-
     calibrated, so a self-consistent MI hydro simulation is needed to finalise. That is now
     the ONLY open escape, and it is a real one -- not a formality.
   * the missing regulator for x -> 0 stays MANDATORY.
====================================================================================================
